  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.68,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "open_connection",
      "components": ["D5", "U6"],
      "pins": ["D5.4", "U6.23", "U6.24"],
      "nets": ["NET0004"],
      "obs": "On the /Microcontroller/ sheet the WS2812B status LED D5 pin 4 (DIN) is wired only to the net NET0004, and NET0004 appears nowhere else on the schematic. The ESP32 U6 pins that would carry the LED data line (GPIO8 pin 23 / GPIO9 pin 24) are drawn with no wire or label. D5 pin 2 (DOUT) is marked no-connect.",
      "expected": "The spec lists a working onboard WS2812B status LED (D1) driven by firmware (led.rs); its DIN must be connected to an ESP32 GPIO so the MCU can clock data into it.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "/Microcontroller/ sheet: D5 WS2812B pin 4 DIN -> NET0004; U6 GPIO8 (23) and GPIO9 (24) have no label/wire; NET0004 has a single endpoint (D5.4)."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "Exposes status over Wi-Fi via a small webserver ... and a single onboard WS2812B status LED (D1)."}
      ],
      "impact": "The status LED data line is floating, so the WS2812B never receives valid data; the onboard status indication described in the spec does not work.",
      "confidence": 0.72
    },
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["J10", "U10", "U5"],
      "pins": ["J10.A4", "U10.2", "U10.1"],
      "nets": ["VBUS", "+5V"],
      "obs": "On the /Microcontroller/ sheet the USB-C receptacle power pin J10 A4 is labelled VBUS, while on the /Power/ sheet the TPS2121 mux U10 IN2 (pin 2) is on the +5V net and its OUT (pin 1) is on the VBUS net (the same name as the USB pin). The +5V net therefore has no driver, and the USB VBUS pin carries the same name as the mux output / buck input rail.",
      "expected": "Spec: USB VBUS pins feed the +5V net which goes to the mux IN2, and the mux OUT is a separate VBUS net. i.e. A4 -> +5V, mux OUT -> VBUS, two distinct nets.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "/Microcontroller/: J10 A4 -> VBUS. /Power/: U10 IN2(2) -> +5V, U10 OUT(1) -> VBUS."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "J12 ... VBUS pins (A4/A9/B4/B9) -> +5V net -> U5 pin 2/2_1 (IN2) ... outputs a single OUT ... onto the VBUS net ... distinct from the USB +5V net upstream of the mux."}
      ],
      "impact": "The USB-C supply is not routed to the mux's IN2 input (the +5V net is undriven), so the autoswitching power path described in the spec is broken; depending on label scope the mux output rail and the USB VBUS pin are also merged, risking back-feed of the unregulated rail into the USB port.",
      "confidence": 0.55
    },
    {
      "category": "short_to_supply",
      "components": ["U4", "U6"],
      "pins": ["U4.1", "U4.2"],
      "nets": ["+3V3", "NET0003"],
      "obs": "On the /Microcontroller/ sheet the TPS1H200 heater switch U4 has a +3V3 connection (junction dot to a +3V3 arrow) at its IN/DIAG_EN pin pair, while only one of those pins carries the GPIO1 control net NET0003.",
      "expected": "Spec: the heater switch is driven by ESP32 GPIO1 on both IN and DIAG_EN; neither control pin should be hard-tied to +3V3.",
      "evidence": [
        {"source": "schematic", "quote": "/Microcontroller/: U4 TPS1H200AQDGNRQ1 pins 1/2 (IN, DIAG_EN) show a +3V3 junction together with the NET0003 label."},
        {"source": "spec", "quote": "driven by ESP32 GPIO1 on both IN and DIAG_EN"}
      ],
      "impact": "With the switch's IN tied to +3V3 instead of GPIO1, the heater output is permanently enabled and GPIO1 can no longer switch the load, defeating the bang-bang heater control.",
      "confidence": 0.45
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The build deviates from the reference in at least the WS2812B status LED, whose DIN net (NET0004) is left unconnected to any ESP32 GPIO, and in the USB/mux power net naming (+5V undriven, VBUS merged with the mux output), with a likely additional mis-tie of the heater switch IN/DIAG_EN to +3V3; the thermocouple SPI topology and buck/feedback wiring otherwise match the spec."
}

